impossible.  \n\nSo go for **All Types** \u2014 I already have W, B, T (exposed), and honors, so keep B1 to maintain All Types requirement? Actually All Types needs all three suits + winds + dragons: I have W, B, T (in meld), F winds, but no dragons yet. If no dragons in final hand, All Types not scored.  \n\nBut dragons: J1 gone, J2:3 left, J3:3 left. Could draw one later.  \n\nBut easier: drop B1 because it\u2019s isolated and go for mixed hand.  \n\nCheck possible quick win: I\u2019m far from ready, need several draws.  \n\nGiven safety and flexibility:  \n\n**F2** is a single wind, not my pair, not easy to complete pung, discard it reduces honor clutter, keeps pairs of F1 and F3 for possible pungs later.  \n\nAlso F2 is prevalent/seat wind, but with only 1 copy, unlikely to complete pung, so it\u2019s actually a safe discard? Others might be waiting for it for pung/kong, but they haven\u2019t picked F2 much (across played F2 once early). Could be risky but less than discarding F1 which I have a pair of.  \n\n**B1** is safer discard maybe, since B1 already used in next player\u2019s chow.  \n\nComparing:  \nB1 discard: next player had [B1,B2,B3] chow, so unlikely to need another B1 for sequence. Others not showing B1 need.  \n\nF2 discard: someone could be collecting winds for Big Four Winds etc., but with many winds still out (F1:4, F3:4, F4:3 left), less chance they need exactly F2.  \n\nFrom history, previous player played J3, not F2. Next player never played F2. Across played F2 once. So F2 may be safe enough.  \n\nTile efficiency: discard B1 keeps winds for pung chances.  \n\nBut if I discard B1, I lose All Types possibility if I don\u2019t get another B later, but All Types needs dragons anyway, which I don\u2019t have.  \n\nActually, discard B1 seems fine.  \n\n**Final choice:** Discard B1.  \n\nReason:  \n- Reduces a stray suit tile, keeps wind pairs for potential Pung of Honors points.  \n- B1 is relatively safe given next player already used it in a Chow and B2/B3 remaining low.  \n- Keeps W tiles together for possible Chow formations (W1,W2,W3 or W3,W4,W5).  \n- Maintains flexibility for hand shapes with honors.  \n\n**Answer:** Play B1"}
