impler hand: All Pungs (6 points) + possibly Seat Wind (F2 pung not needed). With F3 pung, F1 pung, and one more pung from W1 if we draw third W1, that\u2019s 3 pungs, need one more pung from another tile to get All Pungs.  \n\nBetter to keep W1 pair (so W1 pung possible if draw third W1), keep F3 pung, discard W4 or W5. Which one?  \n\nFrom tile count: W4 unseen=2 left, W5 unseen=2 left. Both similar. W4 is slightly less useful because W3 needed for chow, W3 unseen=3 left. W5 can combine with W4,W6 but W6=0 left, so W5\u2019s only chow chance is W3,W4,W5 (needs W3) or W4,W5,W6 impossible. W4 chow can be W2,W3,W4 or W3,W4,W5 or W4,W5,W6. W2 unseen=1 left, W3=3, W6=0. So W4\u2019s possible chow tiles: W2 (1 left), W3 (3 left). W5\u2019s possible chow tiles: W3 (3 left) or W7 (3 left)? W5,W6,W7 needs W6,W7, W6=0 left, so only W3,W4,W5 (needs W3 and W4, I have W4). So W5 needs W3 to chow with W4. Same as W4 needs W3 to chow with W5. So symmetrical.  \n\nSo choose discard: maybe W4, keep W5 because W5 slightly more central? Unclear.  \n\nAlternatively, discard F3? If discard F3, we break pung, but then we have F3 pair, and we can use F3 as pair later and W1 as part of chow/pung? But W1 is only pair.  \n\nBetter keep F3 pung for All Pungs possibility.  \n\nGiven hand disjointed, simplest is discard F2 as highest risk but also most useless in current shape (single honor not pair).  \n\nI\u2019ll choose Play F2.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nKeeping W1 pair and F3 pung maintains two possible groups for winning hand. F2 is a single honor with low chance to complete pung and is moderately safe to discard based on discard history. Discarding F2 keeps flexibility to draw W3 or another W5/W4 to form chow, or draw another W1 for pung.  \n\n**Answer:** Play F2"}
